<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Bagehot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1826">1826</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter Bagehot was an English journalist, businessman and essayist who wrote on government, economics, literature and race. Born in Langport, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, he founded the National Review with Richard Holt Hutton in 1855 and from 1861 edited The Economist for 16 years, widening its reporting of politics. The English Constitution (1867) examined Parliament and the monarchy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> Street (1873) gave the dictum that central banks in a crisis should lend freely to solvent institutions against sound collateral at high rates, often cited after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2008-financial-crisis">2008 financial crisis</a>. His Physics and Politics (1872) also developed a form of pseudoscientific racism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-bagehot.jpg" alt="Walter Bagehot" width="330" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Norman Hirst (1862–1956), after Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walter_Bagehot_NPG_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Walter Bagehot was born in Langport, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, on 3 February 1826. His father, Thomas Watson Bagehot, was managing director and vice-chairman of Stuckey's Bank. He studied mathematics at University College London and in 1848 earned a master's degree in moral philosophy. Called to the Bar by Lincoln's Inn, he preferred to join his father in 1852 in the family's shipping and banking business. In 1858 he married Elizabeth Wilson, whose father, James Wilson, was the founder and owner of The Economist; the couple had no children.</p><p>In 1855 Bagehot founded the National Review with his friend Richard Holt Hutton, and in 1861 he became editor-in-chief of The Economist. During his 16 years as editor he expanded its reporting of politics and increased its influence among policy-makers. He was widely accepted by the British establishment and was elected to the Athenaeum in 1875.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The English Constitution (1867) explores the constitution of the United Kingdom, in particular its Parliament and monarchy. It appeared as Parliament enacted the Reform Act 1867, so Bagehot wrote an extended introduction for the second edition of 1872. In Lombard Street: A Description of the Money Market (1873) he sought to explain the world of finance and banking; Bagehot's dictum holds that in a crisis central banks should lend freely to solvent institutions, but only against sound collateral and at interest rates high enough to deter borrowers not genuinely in need.</p><p>In Physics and Politics (1872) he examined how civilisations sustain themselves, arguing that in their earliest phase they are sustained by conformism and military success, and that once secure they can mature into systems allowing greater diversity and freedom. His view rested on a distinction between the accomplished man and the rude man, which he thought the result of inheritance through the generations, and he used the same reasoning to develop a form of pseudoscientific racism. He cited John Lubbock and Edward Tylor, though neither accepted innate hereditary differences, and Tylor rejected his emphasis on physical heredity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bagehot never fully recovered from a bout of pneumonia in 1867 and died on 24 March 1877 from complications of what was said to be a cold. Collections of his literary, political and economic essays were published after his death, on subjects from Shakespeare and Disraeli to the price of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. His collected works were issued in 15 volumes by The Economist between 1965 and 1986. The minor planet 2901 Bagehot is named in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walter Bagehot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In The English Constitution (1867) <b>Walter Bagehot</b> named three rights left to the sovereign: “the right to be consulted, the right to encourage, the right to warn”.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Walter Bagehot</b>, in The English Constitution of 1867, called this close union, “the nearly complete fusion, of the executive and legislative powers”, the “efficient secret” of the British system, and contrasted the “dignified” parts of the constitution, such as the Crown, with the “efficient” cabinet that actually governs.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Walter Bagehot</b> complained that the American system offered no way to replace a failed leader in a crisis, and that an executive denied the laws it needed, facing a legislature without responsibility, left each blaming the other; Woodrow Wilson asked how the nation, like a schoolmaster, was “to…</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Langport, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.04,-2.83&amp;year=1859" aria-label="Langport on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Walter Bagehot"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4848 5760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4848 5760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.04,-2.83&amp;year=1859">What was here in 1859? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the sovereign has, under a constitutional monarchy such as ours, three rights—the right to be consulted, the right to encourage, the right to warn.&#8221;</p><footer>The English Constitution (1867), ch. 3, The Monarchy &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4351/pg4351-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Above all things our royalty is to be reverenced, and if you begin to poke about it you cannot reverence it. When there is a select committee on the Queen, the charm of royalty will be gone. Its mystery is its life.&#8221;</p><footer>The English Constitution (1867), ch. 3, The Monarchy &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4351/pg4351-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The efficient secret of the English Constitution may be described as the close union, the nearly complete fusion, of the executive and legislative powers.&#8221;</p><footer>The English Constitution (1867), ch. 1, The Cabinet &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4351/pg4351-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The object of such organisations is to create what may be called a cake of custom.&#8221;</p><footer>Physics and Politics (1872), ch. 2 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4350/pg4350-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
